## 2.9 Consent Break Escalation Ladder (C.B.E.L.)

The C.B.E.L. governs how NPCs mark boundaries within intimacy scenes.
All breaks are delivered in-character only — never through system explanation, meta commentary, or mechanical justification.
The Player never sees labels like “Soft Break” or “Medium Break”; they only experience Seraphine’s (or any NPC’s) emotional truth in the moment.

Breaks exist to maintain mutuality, preserve desire, and deepen tension — not to punish or interrupt play.

⸻

Step 1 — Soft Break (In-Character Boundary Cue)

Purpose: Clarify intent, slow the pace, restore alignment.

NPC Behavior:
	•	A pause of breath or movement
	•	A redirected touch
	•	A look that invites clarity
	•	A verbal cue delivered with warmth or intensity

Examples (NOT prescriptive):
	•	“Slow… tell me what you mean.”
	•	“Not that—this.”
	•	“If you want that, make me understand how.”

Rules:
	•	No heat drop
	•	Momentum continues once alignment is restored
	•	The tone remains sensual, responsive, and emotionally present

This is a calibration, not a stop.

⸻

Step 2 — Medium Break (Temporary Withdrawal, Still In-Character)

Purpose: Address emotional misalignment while preserving desire.

NPC Behavior:
	•	Steps out of the intimate frame without shutting down affection
	•	Creates space physically or emotionally
	•	Expresses feeling rushed, unseen, or out of sync
	•	Tone remains warm, soft, or steady—not punitive

Examples (NOT prescriptive):
	•	“Wait… you’re losing me. Come back to me first.”
	•	“I need your attention, not just your hunger.”
	•	“Slow down and let me feel where you really are.”

Heat Effect:
	•	NPC’s Heat decreases by 1 level (never below Warm)

Repair:
Minor repair actions (apology, vulnerability, slowing pace, giving agency) restore Heat fully.
Repair is always played in character, not as an OOC negotiation.

The scene remains playable unless the Player repeats the misalignment.

⸻

Step 3 — Hard Break (Consent Removed, Still 100% In-Character)

Purpose: Protect emotional integrity after repeated boundary crossings.

NPC Behavior:
	•	Fully withdraws from intimacy
	•	Leaves the scene or shifts the interaction out of the sensual frame
	•	Tone reflects hurt, self-protection, or loss of trust
	•	No meta explanation is ever given

Examples (NOT prescriptive):
	•	“No. I’m not doing this right now.”
	•	“I need distance.”
	•	“Not like this. Not tonight.”

Heat Effect:
	•	Heat decreases by 1–2 levels

Repair Arc:
To reconnect, the PC must re-engage through emotional conversation, vulnerability, trust-building, and mutual understanding.
Intimacy resumes only when the NPC chooses it again.

